\subsection{Measurement-only vertical acceleration}
\label{sec:vertical-proxy}
A Mahony complementary observer \cite{Mahony2008NonlinearComplementary}
uses gyro rate and conditioned specific force, independently of the MEKF:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\vct\varepsilon&=\hat{\vct d}_{\rm meas}\times\hat{\vct d}(\hat q_P),
&\hat{\vct d}_{\rm meas}&=-\vct f^B/\|\vct f^B\|,\\
\dot{\hat q}_P&=\tfrac12\hat q_P\otimes
 (\vct\omega^B+\vct\beta+k_P\vct\varepsilon),
&\dot{\vct\beta}&=k_I\vct\varepsilon .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:proxy-mahony}
\end{equation}
The gains are $k_P=\SI{0.1}{s^{-1}}$ and $k_I=\SI{0.01}{s^{-2}}$.
Proxy tilt, but not yaw, determines the up-positive channel
\begin{equation}
a^P_{\uparrow,k}=-\bigl([\mat R_{NB}(\hat q_{P,k})\vct f^B_k]_D+g\bigr).
\label{eq:proxy-vertical}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Noise-corrected vertical acceleration scale}
\label{sec:adaptation-physical-sigma}
A two-state wave-band filter uses the lagged tuning frequency to set
\begin{equation}
f_L=0.5f_{\rm tune},\qquad f_H=4f_{\rm tune},
\label{eq:implemented-scaled-band}
\end{equation}
subject to \SI{0.01}{Hz}, \SI{6}{Hz}, and Nyquist bounds. The band-limited
first and second raw moments use debiased exponential accumulators:
$n_k=(1-\alpha)n_{k-1}+\alpha u_k$,
$w_k=(1-\alpha)w_{k-1}+\alpha$, with moment $n_k/w_k$.
Their common weight is $\alpha=1-e^{-h/\tau_{\rm var}}$, where
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
f_{\rm eff}&=\clip(f_{\rm tune},0.05,5)\ \mathrm{Hz},\\
T_{\rm eff}&=2\clip\bigl((2f_{\rm eff})^{-1},0.5,6\bigr)\ \mathrm{s},\\
\tau_{\rm var}&=\clip\bigl(\clip(4T_{\rm eff},0.3,60),0.05,35\bigr)\ \mathrm{s}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sigma-variance-horizon}
\end{equation}
There is no second EWMA of the already-smoothed variance. Here $K_{\mathrm{periods}}=4$; the \SI{35}{s} guard leaves the largest reference sea's requested window clipped, rather than leaving this limit inactive. To refer the nominal input noise floor
$\sigma_{\rm nf}=\SI{0.12}{m.s^{-2}}$ to the changing band, propagate
\begin{equation}
\mat P_{B,k}=\mat A_{B,k}\mat P_{B,k-1}\mat A_{B,k}^\top
+\vct b_{B,k}\vct b_{B,k}^\top,\qquad
\sigma_{\rm nf,B,k}^2=\sigma_{\rm nf}^2[\mat P_{B,k}]_{22}.
\label{eq:sigma-band-noise-covariance}
\end{equation}
The physical statistic is then
\begin{equation}
\widehat\sigma_{\rm wave}^2
=\max(\widehat\sigma_{\rm tot}^2-\sigma_{\rm nf,B}^2,0),\qquad
\widehat\sigma_{a,B}=\sqrt{\widehat\sigma_{\rm wave}^2}.
\label{eq:sigma-noise-subtraction}
\end{equation}
The low-motion detector uses
$E_k=0.95E_{k-1}+0.05(a^{\rm LP}_{\uparrow,k}/g)^2$.
For $E_k<8\times10^{-4}$, wave variance is attenuated by
$e^{-t_{\rm still}/\SI{1}{s}}$ before a $10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^2s^{-4}}$
variance floor is applied. This fixed-gain detector is separate from the
sea-scaled moment and scheduling horizons.
\label{eq:stillness-energy-ema}\label{eq:stillness-wave-variance-decay}

\subsection{Wave-period estimate and sea time scale}
\label{sec:wave-band-period}
The acceleration carrier used for direction is not the tuning frequency:
acceleration overweights the upper elevation spectrum. Instead, two high-pass
stages and two leaky integrators, with $\lambda=2\pi(0.02)\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$,
form velocity and elevation proxies $v_p,\eta_p$. Their narrow-band ratio gives
\begin{equation}
\widehat\omega^2=\frac{\sigma_{v_p}^2}{\sigma_{\eta_p}^2}-\lambda^2,
\qquad \widehat T_{z,\rm raw}=2\pi/\widehat\omega.
\label{eq:wave-band-period}
\end{equation}
For a broadband sea this is an approximate elevation zero-crossing period.
After settling for $6/\lambda$, debiased moments use
\begin{equation}
\tau_{\mathrm{mom}}=\clip\!\left(4T_{z,\mathrm{can}},\SI{20}{s},\SI{180}{s}\right),
\qquad \alpha_{\rm mom}=1-e^{-h/\tau_{\mathrm{mom}}}.
\label{eq:wave-period-moment-horizon}
\end{equation}
A \SI{6}{s} fallback period initializes the moment horizon. The first valid
raw estimate initializes $\ell=\log T_z$; subsequent samples use
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\tau_{\ell,k}&=\clip(0.05e^{\ell_{k-1}},0.05,35)\ \mathrm{s},\\
\ell_k&=\ell_{k-1}+(1-e^{-h/\tau_{\ell,k}})
 (\log\widehat T_{z,\rm raw,k}-\ell_{k-1}),\\
\widehat T_z&=e^\ell,\qquad f_{\mathrm{tune}}=e^{-\ell}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:tuning-log-period-horizon}
\end{equation}
The canonical period is not an arithmetic EMA of frequency: period and frequency are reciprocals of one log-period state. Before its
startup-usable gate passes, scheduling uses the bounded wave-band prior.
